1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 Nbd7 6. g3 a6 7. Bg2 e6 8. O-O
Be7 9. b3 Qc7 10. Bb2 O-O 11. Kh1 Rd8 12. Qe2 Nf8 13. f4 Rb8 ({White has
secured a big advantage from the opening; Black’s game is very cramped and} 
13... b5 {could not be played at once because of the reply} 14. e5) 14. Rad1
Bd7 15. e5 Ne8 16. Nf5 $1 {A simple stroke yet one which brilliantly exploits
his more harmonious deployment.} Ng6 ({Of course, if} 16... exf5 17. Nd5 Qa5
18. Nxe7+ Kh8 19. Qh5 {with a fierce attack (} g6 20. e6+ {etc)}) 17. Nxe7+
Nxe7 18. Ne4 d5 {Forced by the threat of 19 exd6 Nxd6 20 Be5.} 19. Nd6 Qa5 ({
If Black tried} 19... Nxd6 20. exd6 Qxd6 {, White would win the exchange for a
pawn by} 21. Be5 {. This appears to us, however, far his best chance of
holding the game.}) 20. Qh5 Nxd6 21. exd6 Nf5 22. g4 Nxd6 23. f5 Qxa2 {A
pointless move which renders an already desperate situation quite hopeless.} (
23... Ne8 {offered a little hope; e.g.,} 24. fxe6 (24. Rd3 $1 $18 {Stockfish})
24... Bxe6 25. c4 Qb4 {, so that, if} 26. cxd5 Qxg4 $1 {(but} 27. Qe5 $18 {
Stockfish)}) 24. Be5 Ne8 {It is all over. The natural reply, ...Be8, is out of
the question, because that square is wanted for the knight in answer to Qg5.}
25. fxe6 Bxe6 26. Bxb8 Rxb8 27. Qe5 Rc8 28. Bxd5 Nf6 29. Bxe6 Re8 30. Bxf7+
Kxf7 31. Rxf6+ {Source: tournament book; Illustrated London News, 29 April
1950 (notes by BH Wood)} (31. Rxf6+ {A suitable alternative to playing} gxf6
32. Rd7+ $1) 1-0

