1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. Qc2 c5 5. e3 O-O 6. Nf3 d5 7. dxc5 Bxc5 8. Bd3
Nc6 9. a3 Qe7 10. O-O a5 11. Bd2 Bd6 ({This over-active bishop is largely
responsible for Black's downfall. Quick development by (say)} 11... b6 {
, ...Bb7 and ...Ra to the centre is preferable.}) 12. Rfe1 ({Doubtful, as
White fails to find an opportunity to advance the e-pawn. Better} 12. Rac1)
12... Ne5 13. Nxe5 Bxe5 14. h3 Bb8 {Black suffers later for shutting the
a8-rook out of play.} 15. cxd5 exd5 16. Ne2 ({This, played to meet the threat
on the diagonal, lets in the white knight,} 16. e4 {is an alternative, with
something like equality, whether the pawn is taken or passed.}) 16... Ne4 17.
Rad1 Bd7 ({Here} 17... Qh4 {would give Black some attack.}) 18. Bc3 Bc6 19. Bd4
{Threatening 20 Bxe4 dxe4 21 Bc5.} Qd6 20. f4 Re8 21. Be5 {Setting a trap into
which Black is enticed.} Rxe5 $2 (21... Qh6 22. Bxb8 Raxb8 23. Nd4 {would
leave the game even.}) 22. fxe5 Qxe5 23. Bxe4 {The point: the bishop cannot be
taken.} f5 {A final blunder, induced by concern for his back line. The end now
comes swiftly.} ({If} 23... Qxe4 24. Qxe4 dxe4 25. Rd8+) ({If} 23... dxe4 24.
Rd8+ Be8 25. Qc8 Kf8 26. Red1 Qh2+ (26... Qb5 27. Kf1) 27. Kf1 Qh1+ 28. Kf2
Bg3+ 29. Nxg3 Rxc8 30. Nxh1) 24. Bxd5+ Bxd5 25. Qc8+ Kf7 26. Qd7+ Kf6 27. Qd8+
Kf7 28. Qxd5+ {Source: CHESS, May 1950, Vol.15/176, p169} 1-0

